      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "value": "@{58c62e995865713647a7115b|full_name}: That is correct. Unity Prefabs typically cannot reference objects inside a scene unless they have some script attached to them to assign those references at runtime.\n\nHowever, Unity's GameObject.Instantiate function is built to not just handle Prefabs, but any game object in general. So a reasonable solution would be to specify the prototype object as a scene object instead of a prefab. Since you are cloning a scene object, the references to other objects in the scene will be preserved.\n\nAs I mentioned, there's a couple of caveats involved. The prototype object must remain disabled so it doesn't get assigned a photon network ID. (otherwise it will cause an error when it gets cloned.) This means that when you spawn it, you have to re-enable it, but that can easily be done on the object itself using the OnSpawn VRC_Trigger.\n\nThe main issue really is that the sdk interface doesn't normally support spawning scene objects. So anytime you click on the SpawnObject event in the inspector it will erase whatever scene object you have specified in there because it isn't a prefab.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2018-04-14T03:49:15.856Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "Dynamically spawned prefabs instantiated using SpawnObject are unable to reference other objects in the world. This severely limits what is possible using spawned objects.\n\nThis feature would allow a disabled world object to be specified in the SpawnObject event's prefab parameter. The spawned object would be clone of the disabled prototype object that gets enabled when spawned. Since the spawned object was instantiated from a world object, all references to the world would also be preserved.",
  "error": null,
